<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monastery of the Cross</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Monastery of the Cross is an Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, built in the eleventh century by the Georgian monk Giorgi-Prokhore on the traditional site where the tree for Christ's cross grew. For centuries it was a centre of Georgian learning in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, and it holds a fresco said to portray the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shota-rustaveli">Shota Rustaveli</a>. It passed to the Greek Orthodox Patriarchate of Jerusalem in 1685.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monastery-of-the-cross.jpg" alt="Monastery of the Cross" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hagai Agmon-Snir حچاي اچمون-سنير חגי אגמון-שניר · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MonasteryOfTheCrossNov15_2022_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
